        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Ask who invented the treadmill and you will get several confident, different answers,
          and most of them are right about something. The machine has been reinvented at least four
          times, each time for a purpose its earlier versions would not recognise.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. When Was the Treadmill Invented? The Short Answer</h2>
<p>
            <strong>The treadmill has no single invention date.</strong> Human- and animal-powered
            treadwheels were used to lift loads and drive machinery in the ancient world, including
            by the Romans. The English engineer William Cubitt introduced the penal treadmill to
            British prisons in 1818. Claude Lauraine Hagen received a US patent for a treadmill-style
            training machine in 1913. The motorised medical treadmill was developed in the 1950s and
            1960s by cardiologist Robert Bruce and engineer Wayne Quinton at the University of
            Washington. And the first home exercise treadmill is generally credited to engineer
            William Staub, who built the PaceMaster in the late 1960s after reading Kenneth
            Cooper's book <em>Aerobics</em>.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the honest answer to "who invented the treadmill" depends on which treadmill you mean.
            If you mean the device you walk on to power something, it is ancient and anonymous. If
            you mean the prison machine the word was coined for, it is Cubitt. If you mean the
            motorised belt machine in your spare room, the lineage runs through Bruce and Quinton to
            Staub.
          </p>
          <p>
            The rest of this page takes those chapters in order, because the strange thing about the
            treadmill is how completely its meaning flipped — from a machine designed to be
            exhausting and pointless as a punishment, to one people pay a few thousand dollars to own
            for precisely the same reason.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="ancient-treadwheels" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Ancient Treadwheels: Walking as Power</h2>
<p>
            Before engines, muscle was the main source of mechanical power, and a person or animal
            walking inside or on a wheel turns out to be an efficient way to deliver it. The basic
            idea — continuous walking converted into rotation — is far older than any exercise
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            The Romans used large treadwheel cranes, in which workers walked inside a wheel to wind
            rope around an axle and lift heavy stone. The mechanical advantage was considerable,
            which is part of how large building projects were possible without engines. The same
            principle reappeared in medieval Europe, where treadwheel cranes were used in building
            cathedrals and in ports, and a handful of historic examples still survive.
          </p>
          <p>
            Animal-powered versions did the same work at smaller scale. Horses, oxen and even dogs
            walked on inclined belts or inside wheels to grind grain, pump water, churn butter or
            drive farm machinery, and such devices were still being sold to farmers into the
            nineteenth century. The inclined animal treadmill — a sloping moving floor that the
            animal walks up while staying in place — is mechanically very close to the modern
            machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            None of this was exercise. The walker was the motor, and the point was the work done at
            the other end of the axle. That changed in 1818.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="penal-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. 1818: William Cubitt and the Prison Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The word "treadmill" in its familiar sense comes from the British prison system. In 1818
            the engineer William Cubitt designed a treadwheel for prisons — reportedly prompted by the
            sight of idle prisoners — as a way of putting them to work. The design was a long
            horizontal cylinder with steps around it, and prisoners stood side by side and kept
            stepping as it turned, like a very long, unforgiving stair climber.
          </p>
          <p>
            Some of these machines did useful work, grinding grain or pumping water, which is where
            the "mill" comes from. Many did nothing at all except turn against a brake, an activity
            described at the time as "grinding the wind". The labour was the point: the treadwheel was
            intended to be hard, monotonous and a deterrent. Shifts could run for hours, with short
            breaks, and the regime was widely regarded as brutal.
          </p>
          <p>
            The treadwheel spread quickly through British prisons in the 1820s and was adopted in some
            American prisons too. It remained a fixture of "hard labour" sentences for much of the
            nineteenth century, though criticism grew steadily, both of its cruelty and its
            pointlessness.
          </p>
          <p>
            Its decline came late in the century. The Prison Act 1898 is generally credited with
            ending the treadwheel as a standard punishment in Britain, though some machines remained in
            use for a few years afterwards before being removed. That is why the word still carries
            its old figurative meaning — a "treadmill" job is one where you work hard and get nowhere.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="hagen-patent" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. 1913: The First Patent for an Exercise Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The first clear sign of the treadmill as a fitness device is a US patent granted in 1913
            to Claude Lauraine Hagen for a "training machine". Hagen's design was a belt-type machine
            intended to let a person run or walk in place, which is recognisably the ancestor of what
            sits in gyms today.
          </p>
          <p>
            There is little evidence that it became a commercial success, and the early twentieth
            century did not produce a home-treadmill industry. Exercise culture as we know it was
            decades away; running for fitness was still unusual, and a large machine dedicated to it
            would have struck most people as eccentric.
          </p>
          <p>
            The patent matters mainly because it is a documented, dateable moment when someone
            designed a treadmill for the person on it rather than for the work it could drive. If
            you want one answer to "when was the exercise treadmill invented", 1913 is the best
            supported, with the caveat that the idea was still waiting for a reason to exist.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="medical-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. 1950s–60s: Robert Bruce, Wayne Quinton and the Medical Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The reason arrived through medicine. Doctors had long understood that heart problems often
            show up only under exertion, and earlier exercise tests used simple step routines. What
            was missing was a way to increase workload smoothly, precisely and repeatably while the
            patient stayed in one place, connected to monitoring equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            At the University of Washington in Seattle, cardiologist Dr Robert Bruce worked with
            engineer Wayne Quinton through the 1950s and into the 1960s on exactly that problem.
            Quinton built motorised treadmills whose speed and incline could be controlled reliably,
            and Bruce developed the exercise protocol that ran on them. The Bruce protocol, published
            in 1963, uses three-minute stages in which both speed and grade rise, and it became the
            standard way to conduct a treadmill exercise test — it is still widely used today, as our
            <a href="/treadmill-stress-test/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill stress test explainer</a>
            describes.
          </p>
          <p>
            Quinton went on to found a company making medical treadmills and related equipment. In
            hindsight this period did more for the modern treadmill than any earlier patent: it
            produced a motorised, belt-driven, incline-capable machine with a control system, and it
            gave the treadmill scientific legitimacy as a way of measuring and improving
            cardiovascular fitness.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="home-treadmill" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Late 1960s: William Staub and the First Home Treadmill</h2>
<p>
            The jump from clinic to home is usually credited to William Staub, a mechanical engineer
            in New Jersey. The story most often told is that Staub read Kenneth Cooper's 1968 book
            <em>Aerobics</em> — which argued for regular cardiovascular exercise and helped popularise
            the very word — and decided to build an affordable treadmill so people could follow that
            advice at home.
          </p>
          <p>
            The result was the PaceMaster, introduced in the late 1960s, generally described as the
            first treadmill designed and sold for home use. Accounts differ on the exact model names
            and years, so treat precise dates with caution, but the outline is consistent across
            sources. Staub is also reported to have kept using his own treadmills well into old age,
            which is a better advertisement than most.
          </p>
          <p>
            Cooper's influence is hard to overstate. <em>Aerobics</em> arrived just before the
            running boom of the 1970s, when jogging went from oddity to mainstream pastime in the
            United States. A machine that let you run regardless of weather or darkness suddenly had a
            market.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="timeline" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. The Treadmill Timeline at a Glance</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Ancient world:</strong> treadwheel cranes and animal-powered treadmills
            convert walking into lifting and milling power. The Romans used treadwheel cranes in
            construction.</li>
            <li><strong>Medieval to 19th century:</strong> treadwheel cranes in building and ports;
            animal treadmills on farms.</li>
            <li><strong>1818:</strong> William Cubitt introduces the penal treadmill to British
            prisons.</li>
            <li><strong>1820s:</strong> the prison treadwheel spreads across Britain and to some US
            prisons.</li>
            <li><strong>1898:</strong> the Prison Act is generally credited with ending its use as a
            standard British punishment.</li>
            <li><strong>1913:</strong> Claude Lauraine Hagen is granted a US patent for a
            treadmill-style training machine.</li>
            <li><strong>1950s–60s:</strong> Robert Bruce and Wayne Quinton develop the motorised
            medical treadmill; the Bruce protocol is published in 1963.</li>
            <li><strong>1968:</strong> Kenneth Cooper's <em>Aerobics</em> is published.</li>
            <li><strong>Late 1960s:</strong> William Staub's PaceMaster, generally regarded as the
            first home treadmill.</li>
            <li><strong>1970s–80s:</strong> the running boom; treadmills spread into health clubs.</li>
            <li><strong>1990s–2000s:</strong> folding frames, programmed workouts and heart-rate
            features bring treadmills into ordinary homes in large numbers.</li>
            <li><strong>2010s–2020s:</strong> touchscreens, streamed classes and subscriptions; a
            pandemic-era surge in home demand; walking pads and under-desk machines.</li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div id="mainstream" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. 1970s–2000s: From Health Club to Spare Room</h2>
<p>
            Through the 1970s and 1980s treadmills became standard equipment in health clubs, and
            commercial machines got sturdier, quieter and better at holding speed under a running
            user. Home machines existed, but they were expensive and large, and the cheaper ones were
            often unpleasant to use.
          </p>
          <p>
            The 1990s and 2000s are when the treadmill became an ordinary household purchase. Several
            changes arrived together: more efficient motors, electronic consoles with programmed
            workouts, pulse grips and chest-strap heart-rate support, and — crucially for homes —
            folding frames with lift assistance. Prices fell into a range many households could
            justify, and for years the treadmill has been widely reported as the most popular piece
            of home cardio equipment.
          </p>
          <p>
            Most of the specifications buyers compare today date from this era: continuous-duty
            horsepower, deck size, cushioning, incline range and user weight limit. If you are
            weighing those up, our
            <a href="/treadmill-buying-guide-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill buying guide</a>
            explains which ones matter, and the
            <a href="/manual-vs-motorized-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">manual vs motorized comparison</a>
            covers the non-motorised machines that never quite went away.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connected-era" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. The Connected and Subscription Era</h2>
<p>
            The most recent chapter is about screens and software. From the 2010s, premium treadmills
            gained large touchscreens, internet connections, streamed instructor-led classes and
            programmes that adjust speed and incline automatically. Many of those features sit behind
            a monthly subscription, which changed the economics of owning a treadmill: the purchase
            price became the start of the cost rather than all of it.
          </p>
          <p>
            Demand for home equipment surged in 2020, when gyms closed and many treadmills were hard
            to find for months. The same period accelerated two other trends: compact walking pads
            designed for under-desk use, and curved, self-powered commercial treadmills popular with
            sprint and athletic training. Attention to safety grew as well, including after a
            high-profile 2021 recall of a connected treadmill, with features such as rear guards and
            lockouts that stop the belt being started by children getting more scrutiny.
          </p>
          <p>
            Not everyone wants the subscription model, and plenty of good machines work perfectly well
            without one — see our guide to
            <a href="/best-treadmills-without-subscriptions/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmills without subscriptions</a>.
            For the compact end of the market, the
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs treadmill</a>
            comparison explains what you give up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="myths" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Treadmill History Myths Worth Correcting</h2>
<p>
            <strong>"The treadmill was invented as a torture device."</strong> Half true. The
            prison treadwheel was a punishment, and a harsh one, but walking-powered machines had
            been doing useful work for many centuries before Cubitt, and the exercise treadmill
            descends more directly from the medical machine than from the prison one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>"One person invented the treadmill."</strong> No single inventor exists. Cubitt,
            Hagen, Bruce, Quinton and Staub each have a legitimate claim to a different machine.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>"Treadmills were invented for runners."</strong> Running was almost an
            afterthought. The modern machine was shaped first by cardiology and only later by the
            jogging boom, which is why incline has always been a core feature rather than an add-on —
            the Bruce protocol relies on it, and the
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">12-3-30 workout</a>
            is a modern rediscovery of the same idea.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Precise dates for early home machines.</strong> Be wary of very specific claims
            about model names and years for late-1960s home treadmills. The broad story is solid;
            the fine detail varies from source to source.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-it-means" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">11. What the History Means for Buyers Today</h2>
<p>
            Two things from this history are practically useful. First, the motorised treadmill was
            built as a precise instrument, and the qualities that mattered in the clinic — holding a
            set speed under load, a stable deck and a reliable incline — are still what separate a
            good machine from a poor one. A large screen is not a substitute for any of them.
          </p>
          <p>
            Second, the treadmill's oldest job, steady walking, remains its most valuable one for most
            people. The heart researchers who put patients on treadmills were measuring something
            ordinary walking builds, which is why a well-built
            <a href="/best-walking-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking treadmill</a>
            is a perfectly sensible purchase for anyone who never intends to run.
          </p>
          <p>
            The machine has gone from penance to medical instrument to consumer product, and
            the joke writes itself: we now pay to do voluntarily what prisoners were once sentenced
            to. The difference, of course, is the off switch.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>


      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">12. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When was the treadmill invented?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends which treadmill you mean. Walking-powered treadwheels date back to the ancient world, including Roman treadwheel cranes. William Cubitt introduced the prison treadmill in 1818. Claude Lauraine Hagen received a US patent for a treadmill-style training machine in 1913. The motorised medical treadmill was developed in the 1950s and 1960s, and the first home exercise treadmill appeared in the late 1960s.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who invented the treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                There is no single inventor. English engineer William Cubitt designed the penal treadmill used in British prisons from 1818. Claude Lauraine Hagen patented a training machine in the US in 1913. Dr Robert Bruce and engineer Wayne Quinton developed the motorised medical treadmill at the University of Washington, and William Staub is generally credited with the first home treadmill, the PaceMaster, in the late 1960s.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why were treadmills used in prisons?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The prison treadwheel, introduced by William Cubitt in 1818, was meant to put prisoners to hard, monotonous labour as punishment and deterrence. Some ground grain or pumped water, but many did no useful work at all. It was a standard part of hard-labour sentences in Britain for much of the nineteenth century until the Prison Act 1898 is generally credited with ending it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Who invented the home treadmill?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mechanical engineer William Staub is generally credited with building the first home exercise treadmill, the PaceMaster, in the late 1960s. The story usually told is that he was inspired by Kenneth Cooper's 1968 book Aerobics and wanted an affordable machine people could use at home. Exact model names and dates vary between sources.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When did treadmills become popular for exercise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Treadmills spread into health clubs during the 1970s and 1980s, helped by the running boom that followed Kenneth Cooper's Aerobics. They became an ordinary household purchase in the 1990s and 2000s, as folding frames, programmed consoles and lower prices arrived, and saw another surge in home demand in 2020.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What did Robert Bruce have to do with treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Dr Robert Bruce was a cardiologist at the University of Washington who, working with engineer Wayne Quinton, developed the clinical treadmill exercise test. His Bruce protocol, published in 1963, raises speed and incline every three minutes and is still widely used for stress testing. That work gave the motorised treadmill its scientific credibility.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes in Treadmill History</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">The treadmill story is retold constantly, and the same four errors turn up in most versions of it.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Naming a single inventor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">The treadmill was invented several times for different purposes. Crediting Cubitt alone ignores the exercise machine; crediting Staub alone ignores the medical machine he built on. The accurate answer names the machine first, then the person.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating the prison treadmill as the direct ancestor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Cubitt’s treadwheel was a stepped cylinder, closer to a stair climber than to a belt treadmill. The modern machine descends more directly from motorised medical treadmills and earlier belt designs such as Hagen’s patent. The prison story explains the word, not the engineering.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Quoting over-precise dates</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Ancient dates are approximate by nature, and accounts of late-1960s home treadmills disagree on model names and years. Firm dates exist for the 1913 patent, the 1898 Act and the 1963 Bruce protocol; elsewhere, a decade is more honest than a day.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting the medical chapter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Popular histories often jump from prisons straight to home gyms. The 1950s and 1960s work of Robert Bruce and Wayne Quinton is what produced a precise, motorised, incline-capable machine and gave it scientific credibility. Without it the home treadmill would look very different.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

